    "summary": "Iconic Dark Star and debut Box of Rain despite lo‑fi tape",
    "blurb": "The acoustic first set shines with a heartfelt Box of Rain, Ripple and Brokedown Palace. The second set delivers an unforgettable Dark Star that outweighs the muffled audience recording.",
    "review": "September 17, 1970 at the Fillmore East captures a historic moment in Dead history – the very first performance of “Box of Rain” alongside a soaring, extended Dark Star. The acoustic portion, bolstered by Pigpen’s subtle pedal‑steel and piano, feels intimate even through the hiss and crowd chatter; Jerry’s rhythm work and Phil’s vocal lead on “Box of Rain” are repeatedly praised. While the audience tape is undeniably muffled – described as a mic wrapped in a shirt – the energy of the band cuts through, especially during the Dark Star jam where both drummers drive an ecstatic pulse. The setlist flows well, with highlights like “Ripple,” “Brokedown Palace,” and a solid if noisy electric second half that still manages to convey the group’s chemistry. For fans willing to look past the technical flaws, this show offers a rare glimpse of the Dead at a creative crossroads, making it worth a listen for its historic significance and standout moments.",
